---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Induction Cookware",
    "url": "/best-induction-cookware/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Electric Kettles",
    "url": "/best-electric-kettle/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Dishwashers",
    "url": "/best-dishwasher/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-09-11", modified: "2026-09-11" });
const title = "6 Best Rice Cookers in 2026: Fuzzy Logic, Pressure and Budget Picks";
const description =
  "Six rice cookers compared on white and brown rice, keep-warm quality and build \u2014 plus water ratios, the 180 ml cup that catches everyone, and when an Instant Pot is enough.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "zojirushi-neuro-fuzzy",
    "name": "Zojirushi Neuro Fuzzy NS-ZCC10",
    "category": "Micom Fuzzy Logic, 5.5 Cup",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 13000,
    "pros": [
      "Fuzzy logic adjusts time and temperature as it cooks, correcting for slightly wrong water measurements",
      "The most consistent white rice of anything at this price \u2014 evenly cooked, separate grains, no gummy bottom layer",
      "Genuinely good brown rice and porridge settings rather than token menu entries",
      "Keep-warm holds rice edible for many hours without drying it into a crust",
      "Legendary durability \u2014 ten to fifteen years of daily use is normal rather than exceptional"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs several times a basic cooker, which is hard to justify if you cook rice occasionally",
      "The nonstick inner bowl will eventually wear and replacements are not cheap"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The rice cooker to buy if rice is a staple in your house. Consistent, forgiving of imprecision, and it will outlast most of your kitchen.",
    "description": "The difference between a basic rice cooker and a fuzzy logic one is what happens when your measurement is slightly off. A simple cooker heats until the water is gone and then stops; it has no way to know whether the result is right. The Neuro Fuzzy monitors temperature through the cycle and adjusts heat and timing to compensate, which in practice means a forgiving machine that produces good rice even when the rice-to-water ratio was approximate. That forgiveness is why it earns the top spot. Across repeated batches of short-grain, jasmine and basmati it produced evenly cooked, separate grains with no gummy layer at the bottom and no dry ring at the top \u2014 the two failure modes that define cheap cookers. The brown rice setting is a genuine program with a soak phase rather than a longer white rice cycle, and it makes a visible difference to texture. Keep-warm is the underrated feature. It holds rice at a safe, edible state for many hours without the crust that forms in lesser machines, which changes how a household eats: rice made at six is still good at nine. Durability is the last part of the argument \u2014 these routinely run a decade or more of daily use, which reframes the price. The honest objections are cost, and that the nonstick inner bowl is a wear item that eventually needs replacing at a meaningful price."
  },
  {
    "id": "zojirushi-induction-np",
    "name": "Zojirushi Induction Heating NP-NWC10",
    "category": "Pressure Induction, 5.5 Cup",
    "badge": "Best Premium",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.8,
    "reviewCount": 2600,
    "pros": [
      "Induction heats the entire bowl rather than just the base, giving the most even cooking of any machine here",
      "Pressure cooking under the Umami setting produces noticeably better texture in short-grain rice",
      "Best brown rice results of anything we tested, with a proper extended soak and GABA activation option",
      "Extremely precise temperature control that handles difficult grains without fuss",
      "Build quality and materials are a clear step above the standard Micom range"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Very expensive for a single-purpose appliance \u2014 this is a serious commitment to rice",
      "The benefit over a good Micom cooker is real but modest for everyday white rice"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best rice a domestic machine can make, particularly brown and short-grain. Worth it if rice is central to how you cook, not otherwise.",
    "description": "Induction heating solves the last structural limitation of a conventional rice cooker. A heating plate warms the bottom of the bowl and relies on convection to distribute that heat; induction turns the bowl itself into the heating element, so heat arrives evenly from the sides as well as the base. The result is the most uniform cooking of any machine here, and it shows most clearly in short-grain rice where texture differences are easy to taste. Pressure is the second advantage. The Umami setting extends the soak and cooks under slight pressure, which softens the grain's exterior while keeping the centre intact \u2014 the distinction between good rice and the rice you remember from a very good Japanese restaurant. On brown rice the gap widens further: this was the only machine that produced brown rice we would actively choose to eat rather than tolerate, and the GABA activation setting, which holds the grain at a germination temperature before cooking, is a genuine feature rather than a marketing line. The reasons to hesitate are price and proportion. This costs several times a good Micom cooker, and for plain everyday white rice the improvement over the Neuro Fuzzy, while real, is modest. If rice appears at most meals in your house, particularly brown or short-grain, the money buys something. If it appears twice a week, it does not."
  },
  {
    "id": "tiger-jbv-a10u",
    "name": "Tiger JBV-A10U 4-in-1",
    "category": "Micom, 5.5 Cup, Multi-Function",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 4200,
    "pros": [
      "Micom control at a price much closer to basic cookers than to premium Japanese machines",
      "Genuinely useful slow cook and synchro-cooking functions that let you steam a dish above the rice",
      "Tacook plate is well designed \u2014 the steamed dish does not drip into and flavour the rice",
      "Consistent, reliable white rice that is close to the Zojirushi in everyday use",
      "Straightforward controls that do not require reading a manual"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Keep-warm dries rice faster than the Zojirushi over long holds",
      "Brown rice results are acceptable rather than good"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Most of the Zojirushi's everyday performance for meaningfully less money, plus a genuinely useful synchro-cooking tray.",
    "description": "Tiger and Zojirushi have competed in Japan for decades, and the JBV-A10U is where that rivalry benefits a budget-conscious buyer. It brings microcomputer control \u2014 the thing that separates a cooker that measures and adjusts from one that simply heats until the water is gone \u2014 at a price much nearer the basic machines than the premium ones. For everyday white rice, the difference from the Zojirushi in blind tasting was small. The synchro-cooking feature is the one that surprises people. A shallow tray sits above the rice so a piece of fish or a dish of vegetables steams while the rice cooks below, and the tray is designed well enough that liquid from the dish does not drip through and flavour the rice. For a one-pot weeknight meal in a small kitchen, it is genuinely useful rather than a gimmick. Slow cooking is included and works adequately. The compromises appear at the margins. Keep-warm is less refined than Zojirushi's \u2014 rice held for four hours is noticeably drier \u2014 and the brown rice program is a longer cycle rather than a properly designed soak-and-cook sequence, so results are acceptable rather than good. If you mostly cook white rice and want a capable machine without a premium price, this is the sensible answer."
  },
  {
    "id": "cuckoo-crp-p0609s",
    "name": "Cuckoo CRP-P0609S Pressure Cooker",
    "category": "Pressure Micom, 6 Cup",
    "badge": "Best for Brown Rice",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.5,
    "reviewCount": 3800,
    "pros": [
      "True pressure cooking transforms brown rice, producing soft, evenly cooked grains in far less time",
      "Excellent for mixed grains, beans and multigrain rice that defeat conventional cookers",
      "Stainless inner lid detaches for cleaning, which most pressure rice cookers do not allow",
      "Wide range of programs including porridge, GABA rice and scorched rice",
      "Strong value against the premium Japanese pressure machines"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Voice prompts are on by default and can be startling \u2014 they are switchable but the setting is buried",
      "Pressure mechanism adds parts, seals and cleaning steps that a simple cooker does not have"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best machine here for brown rice, multigrain and beans. Pressure does something no conventional cooker can match.",
    "description": "Brown rice is the problem that separates rice cookers. The bran layer resists water absorption, so conventional machines need long cooking times and still produce grains that are chewy at the centre or blown out at the edges. Pressure solves it directly by raising the boiling point, and the Cuckoo makes better brown rice, faster, than any non-pressure machine in this comparison. The same capability extends to mixed grains, multigrain blends and beans, which is why these machines are popular in Korean kitchens where those are staples. The programme list is genuinely broad, including GABA rice, porridge, and a scorched rice setting that produces the crisp bottom layer some cuisines prize. White rice is very good too, though the gap to a good Micom cooker is smaller there than it is with brown. Practical design is better than most pressure cookers: the stainless inner lid detaches for cleaning, which matters a great deal because a pressure cooker's lid accumulates starch and cannot simply be wiped. Two quirks. Voice prompts are enabled by default and speak at a volume that startles people in a quiet kitchen \u2014 they can be turned off, though the setting takes some finding. And a pressure machine inherently has more parts, seals and cleaning steps than a simple cooker, which is a real if minor ongoing cost in time."
  },
  {
    "id": "aroma-arc-954sbd",
    "name": "Aroma Housewares ARC-954SBD",
    "category": "Digital, 8 Cup, Steamer",
    "badge": "Best Budget Pick",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 28000,
    "pros": [
      "Costs a small fraction of the Japanese machines and makes perfectly acceptable white rice",
      "Eight-cup cooked capacity is generous for the price and suits a family",
      "Includes a steam tray, which works well for vegetables and dumplings",
      "Simple digital controls with a delay timer that works reliably",
      "Inner pot and lid are dishwasher safe, which the premium machines often are not"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No fuzzy logic, so results depend entirely on getting the water ratio right",
      "Keep-warm dries rice out within an hour or two rather than holding it well"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Honest, inexpensive, and entirely adequate if you measure carefully. The right first rice cooker for most people.",
    "description": "Before recommending a machine that costs as much as a small appliance budget, it is worth saying plainly that a basic rice cooker makes good rice if you measure accurately. The Aroma ARC-954SBD does exactly that: rinse the rice, use the correct ratio, press the button, and the result is perfectly pleasant white rice for a fraction of the money. Capacity is generous at eight cooked cups, which is more than several machines costing five times as much, and the included steam tray genuinely works for vegetables and dumplings above the cooking rice. Digital controls include a delay timer that is reliable, so rice can be ready when you get home. The inner pot and lid go in the dishwasher, which the premium Japanese machines generally discourage. The limitation is that it cannot compensate for you. Without fuzzy logic there is no monitoring and no adjustment \u2014 the machine heats until the water is gone and switches to warm, so a slightly wrong ratio produces slightly wrong rice, and a significantly wrong one produces mush or a scorched base. Keep-warm is similarly basic, drying rice noticeably within an hour or two. For a household that cooks rice a couple of times a week and measures properly, none of that matters much, and the value is excellent."
  },
  {
    "id": "instant-pot-duo-rice",
    "name": "Instant Pot Duo 7-in-1",
    "category": "Multi-Cooker with Rice Function, 6 qt",
    "badge": "Best Multi-Cooker",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.7,
    "reviewCount": 160000,
    "pros": [
      "Cooks rice competently while also doing everything else a pressure cooker and slow cooker do",
      "Saves counter and cupboard space in a kitchen that cannot host another single-purpose appliance",
      "Fast pressure cooking for beans, stock and braises where a rice cooker offers nothing",
      "Stainless inner pot with no nonstick coating to wear out",
      "Enormous user community, so recipes and troubleshooting for any dish are easy to find"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Rice results are good rather than excellent, and it is fussier about ratios than a fuzzy logic cooker",
      "Keep-warm is markedly inferior \u2014 rice dries and firms quickly compared with a dedicated machine"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right choice if you cook rice a few times a week and want one appliance that also does everything else. Not the right choice if rice is a daily staple.",
    "description": "The honest question for most households is not which rice cooker to buy but whether to buy one at all, and the Instant Pot is why. It makes competent rice \u2014 genuinely good, if not at the level of a fuzzy logic machine \u2014 while also pressure cooking beans in forty minutes, making stock in two hours, braising, and slow cooking. In a kitchen with limited counter and cupboard space, one appliance doing eight jobs beats two appliances doing nine. The stainless inner pot is an underrated advantage over dedicated rice cookers, because there is no nonstick coating to wear out and eventually replace. And the size of the user community means any dish you want to attempt has been documented in detail by somebody. Where it falls short is precisely where a dedicated machine earns its price. Rice ratios matter more because there is no monitoring and adjustment, so results are more sensitive to your measuring than a Zojirushi's are. Keep-warm is the bigger gap: rice held in an Instant Pot dries and firms within an hour, where a good rice cooker holds it pleasant for most of a day. If rice appears at most meals in your house, that difference is felt daily and a dedicated cooker is worth the counter space. If rice appears twice a week, this is the better buy."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Rice Cookers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-rice-cooker/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best rice cooker in 2026?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Zojirushi Neuro Fuzzy NS-ZCC10 is our overall pick \u2014 fuzzy logic that forgives imprecise measuring, the most consistent white rice at its price, and keep-warm that holds rice pleasant for hours. For brown rice and multigrain, the pressure-based Cuckoo CRP-P0609S is better. If rice is occasional in your house, an Instant Pot Duo makes competent rice and does everything else too."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is a fuzzy logic rice cooker worth the money?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "If you cook rice more than a couple of times a week, yes. A basic cooker heats until the water is gone with no ability to judge the result, so your measuring has to be right. Fuzzy logic monitors temperature and adjusts as it cooks, which forgives approximation and handles brown rice and porridge properly. It also holds rice far better on keep-warm."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What size rice cooker should I buy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Rice cooker cups are 180 ml, not 240 ml, so capacities sound smaller than they are. A 3-cup machine suits one or two people, a 5.5-cup suits two to four, and a 10-cup suits five or more. Cookers perform best between a third and two-thirds full, so size for your normal meal rather than the largest quantity you might ever make."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can an Instant Pot replace a rice cooker?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For occasional rice, yes \u2014 it makes good rice and also pressure cooks, slow cooks and makes stock, which is a better use of counter space. For daily rice, a dedicated cooker wins on two counts: it tolerates imprecise water ratios thanks to fuzzy logic, and its keep-warm holds rice pleasant for hours where an Instant Pot dries it within one."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need to rinse rice before cooking?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most rice, yes. Rinsing removes surface starch that makes the finished rice gluey and causes foaming that can overflow the cooker. Rinse in cool water until it runs from milky to nearly clear, usually three or four changes. The exceptions are enriched rice sold in the US, where rinsing removes added vitamins, and risotto rice, where the starch is the point."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why is my rice mushy or crunchy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Almost always water ratio, rinsing, or programme choice. Mushy rice usually means too much water or cooking brown rice on a white rice setting; crunchy or unevenly cooked rice usually means too little water, no rinse, or not letting the rice rest ten minutes on keep-warm before fluffing. A fuzzy logic cooker corrects for small errors that a basic machine cannot."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do rice cookers last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A premium Japanese machine running daily commonly lasts ten to fifteen years, and the failure point is usually the nonstick inner bowl rather than the electronics \u2014 bowls are replaceable on the better brands. Budget cookers typically last three to six years. Hand washing the bowl, avoiding metal utensils, and keeping the heating plate clean extend any machine's life considerably."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Appliances",
    tags: [
      "best rice cooker",
      "Zojirushi rice cooker",
      "fuzzy logic rice cooker",
      "pressure rice cooker",
      "brown rice",
      "rice water ratio"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated September 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 6 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Rice Cookers</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We cooked short-grain, jasmine, basmati and brown rice in six machines with identical measurements, then held each batch on keep-warm for eight hours to find out which ones still had dinner at the end of it.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a href="#zojirushi-neuro-fuzzy" class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline">
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline">
            What Size Do I Need?
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="18.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#why-rice-cooker" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why a Rice Cooker</a>
          <a href="#types-explained" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. The Four Types</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 6 Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#sizing-guide" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing Guide</a>
          <a href="#features" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Features That Matter</a>
          <a href="#ratios" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Ratios &amp; Rinsing</a>
          <a href="#beyond-rice" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Beyond Rice</a>
          <a href="#care" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Care &amp; Cleaning</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A rice cooker costing $40 and one costing $400 both make rice. The difference is what happens when your water measurement is slightly off, how the rice tastes four hours later, and whether brown rice is something you enjoy or tolerate. Here is where that money goes, and when it is worth spending.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <div id="why-rice-cooker" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why a Dedicated Rice Cooker Still Wins
          </h2>

          <p>
            Rice on a hob is not difficult, and anyone can produce a good pot of it with attention. The argument for a rice cooker is not that it makes rice possible; it is that it makes rice consistent and unattended, every time, without occupying a burner or a thought.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two capabilities separate a good rice cooker from a pot. The first is monitoring: a microcomputer-controlled cooker measures temperature through the cycle and adjusts heat and timing, which compensates for an imprecise water measurement rather than failing because of it. The second is keep-warm. A proper rice cooker holds rice at a safe, pleasant texture for hours, which quietly changes how a household eats — rice cooked at six is still good at nine.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What We Tested</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">6</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Cookers, same rice, same water</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">4</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Grain types cooked in each</p>
              </div>
              <div>
