          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Fuzzy logic control.</strong> The single most valuable feature and the reason to spend past the entry level. It measures and adjusts during cooking, which means the machine compensates for your approximation rather than failing because of it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep-warm quality.</strong> Hugely variable and rarely compared. A premium cooker holds rice pleasant for eight hours or more; a basic one dries it within two. If your household eats at staggered times, this is worth more than any other feature on the list.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Inner bowl construction.</strong> A thicker bowl with a durable nonstick coating cooks more evenly and survives longer. Bowls are replaceable but not cheap, so treat coating durability as a real specification — and never use metal utensils in one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A detachable inner lid.</strong> Starch condenses and collects on the lid every time you cook, and a lid that removes for washing is far more hygienic than one you wipe around. This is a genuine quality-of-life difference on a machine used daily.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Programme lists.</strong> Largely marketing past a point. Sushi rice, porridge and brown rice settings are genuinely different cycles worth having. Beyond that, a long menu of grain-specific programmes mostly reflects slightly different times and temperatures you would never notice.
          </p>
