                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Water Ratio</th>
                  <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Short-grain white</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 : 1.1</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rinse until water runs nearly clear</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Jasmine</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 : 1.25</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rinse twice; do not oversoak</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Basmati</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 : 1.5</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Soak 20 minutes for longer grains</td>
                </tr>
                <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Brown</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1 : 2</td>
                  <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Use the brown rice programme, not white</td>
                </tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>

          <p>
            Rinsing is not optional for most rice. It removes surface starch that otherwise makes the finished rice gluey and causes the foaming that leads to overflow. Rinse in cool water, swirling with your fingers, until the water runs from milky to nearly clear — usually three or four changes. The exception is enriched rice sold in the US, where rinsing removes the added vitamins, and risotto rice, where you want that starch.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, let the rice rest. Every machine here benefits from ten minutes on keep-warm after the cycle ends, with the lid closed, before you fluff it. That rest lets moisture equalise through the grains, and skipping it is the most common reason home-cooked rice is wetter at the bottom than the top.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="beyond-rice" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Else a Rice Cooker Does Well
          </h2>

          <p>
            A rice cooker is a precisely controlled pot with a lid, which makes it quietly good at several things beyond rice. Steaming is the obvious one: most machines include a tray, and vegetables, dumplings and fish steam above cooking rice with no additional effort or pans.
          </p>

          <p>
            Porridge and congee are where the programmed cookers shine, because both want a long, gentle cook that is tedious to babysit on a hob and trivially easy to set overnight with a delay timer. Steel-cut oats ready at seven in the morning is one of the genuinely life-improving uses of the delay function.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond that, rice cookers handle quinoa, farro, pearl barley and other grains perfectly well — treat them roughly as you would brown rice and adjust water slightly. Some households use them for steamed cakes, hard-boiled eggs, and even small braises, and the machines tolerate it. A pressure model like the Cuckoo extends the range considerably further into beans and multigrain blends.
          </p>

          <p>
            The one thing to avoid is using a rice cooker as a general deep pot. Oil and high-fat cooking damage the nonstick bowl faster than anything else, and the bowl is the most expensive consumable part of the machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8 -->
        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Care That Makes One Last a Decade
          </h2>

          <p>
            The inner bowl is the part that determines the machine's usable life, and its nonstick coating is the part that fails. Never use metal utensils, never stack anything inside it, and wash it by hand with a soft sponge even where the manufacturer permits a dishwasher — detergent and high heat degrade nonstick faster than hand washing does. Use the plastic paddle supplied.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean the inner lid and the steam vent after every few uses. Starch condenses there each time you cook, and a neglected lid develops an unpleasant smell that transfers to the rice. On machines where the inner lid detaches, this is a thirty-second job; on those where it does not, wipe thoroughly with a damp cloth including the gasket.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wipe the heating plate and the outside of the bowl before each use. A grain of rice or a drop of water trapped between the bowl and the plate creates a hot spot, cooks unevenly, and over time pits the plate. It is the most common cause of a cooker that starts scorching the bottom layer after a couple of years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store the machine with the lid slightly ajar if it lives in a humid kitchen. A sealed cooker put away damp holds moisture against the gasket and the bowl, and that is how a machine develops a stale smell that then transfers into the next batch of rice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, if the rice is suddenly worse and nothing else has changed, check the bowl for scratches and the gasket for damage before blaming the machine. Both are replaceable parts, and on the better brands they are available years after purchase — which is part of what you paid for.
          </p>
        </div>


