                The Zojirushi Neuro Fuzzy NS-ZCC10 is our overall pick — fuzzy logic that forgives imprecise measuring, the most consistent white rice at its price, and keep-warm that holds rice pleasant for hours. For brown rice and multigrain, the pressure-based Cuckoo CRP-P0609S is better. If rice is occasional in your house, an Instant Pot Duo makes competent rice and does everything else too.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a fuzzy logic rice cooker worth the money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you cook rice more than a couple of times a week, yes. A basic cooker heats until the water is gone with no ability to judge the result, so your measuring has to be right. Fuzzy logic monitors temperature and adjusts as it cooks, which forgives approximation and handles brown rice and porridge properly. It also holds rice far better on keep-warm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size rice cooker should I buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Rice cooker cups are 180 ml, not 240 ml, so capacities sound smaller than they are. A 3-cup machine suits one or two people, a 5.5-cup suits two to four, and a 10-cup suits five or more. Cookers perform best between a third and two-thirds full, so size for your normal meal rather than the largest quantity you might ever make.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can an Instant Pot replace a rice cooker?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For occasional rice, yes — it makes good rice and also pressure cooks, slow cooks and makes stock, which is a better use of counter space. For daily rice, a dedicated cooker wins on two counts: it tolerates imprecise water ratios thanks to fuzzy logic, and its keep-warm holds rice pleasant for hours where an Instant Pot dries it within one.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to rinse rice before cooking?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most rice, yes. Rinsing removes surface starch that makes the finished rice gluey and causes foaming that can overflow the cooker. Rinse in cool water until it runs from milky to nearly clear, usually three or four changes. The exceptions are enriched rice sold in the US, where rinsing removes added vitamins, and risotto rice, where the starch is the point.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my rice mushy or crunchy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always water ratio, rinsing, or programme choice. Mushy rice usually means too much water or cooking brown rice on a white rice setting; crunchy or unevenly cooked rice usually means too little water, no rinse, or not letting the rice rest ten minutes on keep-warm before fluffing. A fuzzy logic cooker corrects for small errors that a basic machine cannot.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long do rice cookers last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A premium Japanese machine running daily commonly lasts ten to fifteen years, and the failure point is usually the nonstick inner bowl rather than the electronics — bowls are replaceable on the better brands. Budget cookers typically last three to six years. Hand washing the bowl, avoiding metal utensils, and keeping the heating plate clean extend any machine's life considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Rice Cooker Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most bad rice from a good machine comes down to five habits, and every one of them is easy to fix.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring With a US Cup</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The cup supplied with a rice cooker is 180 ml, not 240 ml, and the water lines inside the bowl assume it. Using a US measuring cup for the rice and then filling to the marked line produces too little water and undercooked rice. Use the cup that came in the box, every time.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Rinse</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unrinsed rice carries surface starch that makes the finished result gluey and causes foaming that can boil over. Three or four changes of cool water takes a minute and is the single biggest improvement most people can make to their rice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Cooking Brown Rice on the White Setting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Brown rice has an intact bran layer that resists water absorption and needs both more water and a longer cycle with a soak. Run it on the white programme and you get grains that are chewy at the centre however much water you add.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Metal Utensils in the Bowl</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The nonstick inner bowl is the most expensive consumable part of the machine and metal ruins it. Use the supplied plastic paddle, hand wash with a soft sponge, and do not stack other pans inside the bowl in the cupboard.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Fluffing Immediately at the Beep</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rice needs ten minutes resting on keep-warm with the lid closed so moisture equalises through the grains. Opening and stirring at the beep is the most common reason home-cooked rice ends up wetter at the bottom than the top.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

